{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>My bow I have set in the cloud.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): the reason of the bow is that it be a sign, in the sense of (Genesis 31:52) ״עד הגל הזה ועדה המצבה״, and likewise (Genesis 21:30) ״כי את שבע כבשות תקח מידי בעבור תהיה לי לעדה״ — for every visible thing set before two parties to call to mind a matter is what is called a sign (אות), just as we find that the Torah mentioned it regarding circumcision, as it is said (Genesis 17:11) ״והיה לאות ברית ביני וביניכם״, to call to mind the matter that all the seed of Abraham enter into the covenant of the faith of unity; so too the bow is a sign to remind the generations that the Holy One, blessed be He, does not bring a flood upon the world. And the word נתתי ('I have set') includes either 'I have set now' or 'I have already set it from the beginning.' And there is no doubt that the sign of the bow — whether it existed from the six days of Creation or whether it was now newly made for Noah — for that giving is only to call to mind the conditional matter, that no flood shall come again to destroy the earth. And one may interpret what it says, נתתי, as if it said 'I have sworn,' for the gift of the Holy One, blessed be He, by utterance alone, is established as a sworn oath. And the proof is from the gift of the land to Abraham, of which the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him, ״לזרעך נתתי את הארץ הזאת״; and we find several verses in the Torah that employ the language of swearing, as it is said, ״אשר נשבעתי לאברהם ליצחק וליעקב״, and it is written, ״אשר נשבע ה׳ לאבותיכם לתת להם״ — and in the essence of the promise there is no oath at all, but only the language of gift. And so we may understand here regarding the sign of the bow: when it says ״את קשתי נתתי״, it is as if it said 'I have sworn'; and from this the prophet, peace be upon him, said (Isaiah 54:9) ״כי מי נח זאת לי אשר נשבעתי״ — and there is no language of oath there but only the language of gift. And even though the Sages, of blessed memory, derived from here: לאו לאו תרי זמני שבועה הוי — 'no, no, twice means it is an oath' — for it says ״ולא יכרת כל בשר עוד ממי המבול״ and ״ולא יהיה עוד מבול לשחת הארץ״, the words of Torah are compared to fire and are divided into many meanings. And you will find from the wisdom of Onkelos that he translated ״לזרעך נתתי״ as יהבית ('I have given'), and likewise here, ״את קשתי נתתי״, as יהבית — because His gift, blessed be He, is established as though it had already passed, whereas regarding Abraham, of whom it is written ״נתתי כסף השדה קח ממני״, he translated it as אתן ('I will give') — and even though the money was present in his hand and he did not wish to retract, since it had not yet been given it is not complete until he gives it, lest he retract, for he is flesh and blood; but it is not so with the Holy One, blessed be He, with whom there is no retraction, and therefore he translated it as יהבית — for even the future is expressed in the past tense once He promises it." "<b>And know</b> that this sign does not appear on every cloudy day or every day on which rains fall, but only at a time when the generation is liable [to punishment], in order to make known that He keeps His promise. And you may know that this is so, from what the Sages, of blessed memory, drashed: that the bow was not seen in the generation of Hezekiah, nor in the generation of Rabbi Simeon bar Yohai, because they were perfect righteous ones, and each of them shielded his generation, and therefore they did not need this sign. And likewise they said of Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi that the bow was not seen in his days. And from this you will find that לדורות עולם ('for all generations') is written דרת — missing the vav — and immediately after it comes ״את קשתי״, to teach you that the sign of the bow is only in a time of liability and deficient generations." "<b>And by way of reason (sekhel)</b> the bow is an instrument of war, and it is called קשת because it does not shoot arrows unless a person bends (מקשה) the excess in it; and when it is seen in the sky, only its legs are seen below, and if its legs were above it would be a sign of war, as if from the heavens they were shooting with it upon the earth; but now, since its legs are below and it appears inverted, it is a covenant and it is a sign of peace — and moreover, the strings for shooting arrows are not seen in it. And so is the custom of the nations in time of war, to hold the bow in their hand facing them; and when they call for peace they turn the bow around and hold it by the back of the bow facing them. And the philosophers wrote in their books that the matter of the bow is a natural phenomenon, and they said: the bow has three appearances — the first circle is red, the second is green, the third is redness tending toward blackness; and this third appearance they call red muski, for it is like the color of musko, which is red tending toward blackness. And they said that the material of the bow is a thick cloud, and the cause of the formation of the bow is a spark of the sun striking the thick moist cloud, which is of the species of fine drizzle after rain in most cases; and therefore the cloud, which is black, receives a red appearance — for so does every white appearance, when it passes through blackness, naturally produce from it a red appearance. And the proof of this is damp wood: when it is kindled in fire, the flame is red, because fire tends toward the appearance of whiteness and redness, and it passes through blackness, which is the smoke, which is black, and therefore the flame turns out red." "<b>And from what</b> the Sages, of blessed memory, drashed — ״את קשתי״, 'a form of entrapment (מוקש) to Me' — it is possible that this alludes to the soul, which is likened to the Holy One, blessed be He; and from this it says ״והיה בענני ענן על הארץ״, that is, when I array the mounting of matter, ״ונראתה הקשת בענן״ — the powers of the soul will need to be seen and revealed in the body; this is ״ונראתה הקשת בענן״, for it is the screen that separates between it and the intellect." "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah:</b> ״את קשתי״ — from the language of קושי (hardship) and din (judgment), and it is the attribute of judgment; He said that it shall be a sign of covenant, and this sign is circumcision. And Scripture did not say ״את הקשת נתתי בענן״ but ״את קשתי״, so that the word קשת be sealed with the letter yod at the end; and this is what it says — ״בענן״, that is, the known one, for this cloud is not of the clouds that empty rain, since the bow is seen many times with clear skies; rather, it is the cloud of glory, of which Scripture said (Exodus 16:10) ״והנה כבוד ה׳ נראה בענן״. And this is what it says: ״ונראתה הקשת בענן״, that is, the attribute of judgment is seen in the cloud. And because Scripture said (Ezekiel 1:28) ״כמראה הקשת אשר יהיה בענן ביום הגשם כן מראה הנוגה סביב הוא מראה דמות כבוד ה׳״, the Sages, of blessed memory, drashed: whoever gazes at the bow — his eyes grow dim. And you should rouse your thoughts to the saying found in the Midrash of the Sages, of blessed memory, on this verse ״את קשתי נתתי בענן״, and this is their saying: ״קשתי״ — a thing that is a trap (מוקש) to Me; it is possible, thus, except for קשין דפיריא." "<b>And set.</b> Apply your heart to understand this, and look at the closing of the parashah, where he mentioned a second time זאת אות הברית. And to Noaḥ alone a distinct utterance was reserved in this matter, for above He made known to him and to his sons the matter of the rainbow according to its plain sense, while here He revealed to him alone the matter by way of the secret — and this is ״ויאמר אלהים אל נח זאת אות הברית״."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:11:55.912405508Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}